## Goroutines & Channels in Go

**Goroutine** — a lightweight thread managed by the Go runtime. Launch one with `go`:

```go
go doWork()   // runs concurrently
```

**Channel** — a typed conduit for goroutines to communicate and synchronize. Create one with `make`:

```go
ch := make(chan int)   // unbuffered (blocks until receiver is ready)
ch := make(chan int, 3) // buffered (blocks when full)
```

### How they work together

- `ch <- value` — **send** (blocks until someone receives, or buffer has room)
- `value := <-ch` — **receive** (blocks until data is available)
- `close(ch)` — signals no more values; receivers get the zero value after drain

### Example — fan-out / fan-in

```go
package main

import (
	"fmt"
	"sync"
)

func producer(ch chan<- int) {
	for i := 0; i < 5; i++ {
		ch <- i // send
	}
	close(ch)
}

func worker(id int, in <-chan int, out chan<- int, wg *sync.WaitGroup) {
	defer wg.Done()
	for v := range in { // receives until channel is closed
		out <- v * v // process & forward
		fmt.Printf("worker %d: %d -> %d\n", id, v, v*v)
	}
}

func main() {
	jobs := make(chan int)
	results := make(chan int, 5)
	var wg sync.WaitGroup

	go producer(jobs) // 1 producer goroutine

	// 3 worker goroutines competing for jobs
	for i := 1; i <= 3; i++ {
		wg.Add(1)
		go worker(i, jobs, results, &wg)
	}

	// close results once all workers finish
	go func() {
		wg.Wait()
		close(results)
	}()

	// collect results
	for r := range results {
		fmt.Println("result:", r)
	}
}
```

### Key mental model

| Concept | Analogy |
|---|---|
| Goroutine | A worker you hire with `go` — nearly free (2–4 KB stack) |
| Unbuffered channel | A handoff — sender waits until receiver is ready |
| Buffered channel | A mailbox — sender drops off up to N items without waiting |
| `close(ch)` | "No more mail" — `range ch` exits cleanly |
| `select` | A switch that waits on *multiple* channel operations |

**Don't communicate by sharing memory; share memory by communicating.** — Go proverb. Channels are the primary synchronization mechanism, replacing mutexes in many patterns.
